<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baha al-Din al-Amili</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1547">1547</a>–<a href="/years/1621">1621</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sheikh Bahai, Sheikh Bahāʾī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baha al-Din al-Amili, commonly known as Sheikh Bahāʾī, was a Twelver Shia scholar and polymath of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, closely tied to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>. Born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, he moved as a child to Iran, where his father served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-ul-islam">shaykh al-Islam</a> in several cities to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a>. He wrote over a hundred treatises in Arabic and Persian on jurisprudence, logic, astronomy, mathematics and theology, as well as Persian verse, and seventy-seven scholars are counted among his students. As chief advisor to Abbas I he conceived the urban vision behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and helped begin the planning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-mosque">Shah Mosque</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-qapu">Ali Qapu</a> Palace.</p></div>
